course: books
lesson: 95
title: Just So Stories, The Cat that Walked by Himself, part 3: the third word, the Man, the Dog, and how cats really came to live with us
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: Very angry now, the Woman makes a Still Magic so that she will never say a third word of praise. The Cave grows so still that a little mouse creeps out. «Ouh! Chee!» The Woman jumps onto a footstool and begs the Cat to eat it. He catches it in one jump. «A hundred thanks... You must be very wise,» she says, and the milk-pot cracks in two: the Cat is lapping the milk.
explain: The Woman laughs and gives him a bowl of milk, but warns him: his bargain was not with the Man or the Dog. That evening the Man sets out his two boots, his little stone axe, a piece of wood and a hatchet: if the Cat does not catch mice, he will throw them at him. The Cat agrees, but adds «still I am the Cat who walks by himself», so the Man says he will throw his boots and axe whenever he meets him.
explain: The Dog makes his bargain too: be kind to the Baby or be bitten. The Cat agrees, as long as the Baby does not pull his tail too hard, but again says he walks by himself, so the Dog will chase him up a tree. The Man throws his boots and axe, and the Dog chases the Cat up a tree.
explain: And so, says Kipling, cats catch mice and are kind to babies, but at night they go out to the Wet Wild Woods and the Wet Wild Roofs, waving their wild tails and walking by their wild lones. In the poem, a child likes his dog Binkie better than the Pussy, because Binkie is his true First Friend.
fact: The Still Magic is «not a Singing Magic»; the Cave grows so still that «a little wee-wee mouse crept out of a corner».
fact: The Woman cries «Ouh! Chee! No indeed!», jumps on the footstool and braids up her hair «for fear that the mouse should run up it».
fact: The third word: «A hundred thanks. Even the First Friend is not quick enough to catch little mice as you have done. You must be very wise.» The milk-pot «cracked in two pieces, ffft».
fact: The three magic signs: the curtain fell «whoosh!», the smoke came down «puff!», the milk-pot cracked «ffft».
fact: The Woman: «O Cat, you are as clever as a man, but remember that your bargain was not made with the Man or the Dog.» The Cat: «What is that to me?»
fact: The Man's five things: «his two leather boots and ... his little stone axe (that makes three) and ... a piece of wood and a hatchet (that is five altogether)».
fact: «Not when I am near,» say both the Man and the Dog, when the Cat insists he still walks by himself.
fact: The Dog's bargain: «If you are not kind to the Baby while I am in the Cave ... I will hunt you till I catch you, and when I catch you I will bite you.» The Cat first counts the Dog's teeth (they look very pointed), then agrees.
fact: Kipling's ending: «three proper Men out of five will always throw things at a Cat whenever they meet him, and all proper Dogs will chase him up a tree». Throwing things at animals is cruel; today it is against the law in many countries, and kind people never do it.
fact: The poem: «But Binkie, he snores at my feet all night, And he is my Firstest Friend!»; the child thinks the Pussy's love is «only pretend» because she goes out at night.
fact: Real life: cats came to live near people about 10,000 years ago in the Middle East, when farmers stored grain; the grain drew mice, and the mice drew wildcats. In a way, cats tamed themselves.
fact: Real life: a cat was buried beside a person on the island of Cyprus about 9,500 years ago; the ancient Egyptians loved cats and even made statues of a cat-goddess.
fact: Real life: many cats and dogs who grow up together are good friends; dogs do not always chase cats, and cats are not cruel or false, just more independent.
fact: Talk in this lesson: «it's giving» = it seems like; «bostin'» (Black Country) = brilliant; «eh» (Canada) = right? isn't it?
exercise: What kind of Magic does the Woman make now? | A Still Magic | A Singing Magic, the third and loudest in the world | A warm Fire Magic | A white Milk Magic
exercise: What creeps out when the Cave is so still? | A little wee-wee mouse | The upside-down Bat, looking for the Cat to tell him the news | A snake | A beetle
exercise: What does the Woman do when she sees the mouse? | Jumps on the footstool and braids her hair | Throws her boots at it, like the Man will do later | Calls the Dog | Laughs
exercise: Why does she braid up her hair so quickly? | For fear the mouse should run up it | Because it is bedtime and her hair always gets in the way of the spinning | To look pretty | To make a Magic
exercise: What is the third word of praise? | «You must be very wise» | «You are as beautiful as the Moon over the Wet Wild Woods» | «Good Cat» | «Go away»
exercise: What happens to the milk-pot? | It cracks in two pieces, ffft | It falls into the fire and all the warm white milk boils away | It fills up | It floats
exercise: What does the Woman do after the third word? | Laughs and sets him a bowl of milk | Chases him out of the Cave with her spindle-whorl and her spinning-wheel | Cries | Calls the Man
exercise: What does she warn the Cat? | His bargain was not with the Man or the Dog | He must catch all the mice in the Wet Wild Woods, not only the ones in the Cave | He must leave | He must not drink too much
exercise: What does the Cat answer? | «What is that to me?» | «I am frightened of them» | «I will go and make a bargain with them at once» | «I will go and tell the Bat»
exercise: What does the Man set out in a row? | Two boots, a stone axe, a piece of wood and a hatchet | A bow, three arrows and a spear with a black handle | A bone | A milk-pot
exercise: What is the Man's bargain? | Catch mice, or he'll throw things | Guard the Cave every night, or he will take the warm white milk away for ever | Leave | Be kind to the Baby
exercise: Why does the Man say he will throw things whenever he meets the Cat? | The Cat still says he walks by himself | The Cat stole the milk | The Cat scratched him on the hand when he came home | The Baby cried
exercise: What does the Dog make the Cat promise? | To be kind to the Baby | To catch mice | To stay out of the Cave all night and never come back in | To share his milk
exercise: What does the Cat add to his promise to the Dog? | As long as the Baby doesn't pull his tail too hard | As long as the Dog shares his roast bones with him every evening | Only on Sundays | Nothing
exercise: What does the Cat count before agreeing with the Dog? | The Dog's teeth | The Man's two boots | The empty milk-pots | The mice in the Cave
exercise: What does the Dog say he will do now? | Hunt the Cat up a tree whenever he meets him | Bite the Baby | Leave the Cave for ever and go back to the Wet Wild Woods | Nothing
exercise: What does the Man throw at the Cat in the end? | His two boots and his little stone axe | All five things, the boots, the axe, the wood and the hatchet together | Milk | Nothing
exercise: Where does the Cat go when the moon gets up? | Out to the Wet Wild Woods and Roofs | To sleep in the Baby's cradle, curled up warm beside the fire at the back of the Cave | Into the river | To the Moon
exercise: In the poem, what is the child's dog called? | Binkie | Dingo | Rover | Taffy
exercise: Why does the child think Pussy's love is «only pretend»? | She runs out in the yard all night | She scratches him every morning when he tries to stroke her | She bites | She hides
exercise: Looking back: in part 2, what did two words of praise give the Cat? | A place by the warm fire | A place inside the Cave | The warm white milk three times a day | A name
exercise: Looking back: in part 1, what was the Dog's new name? | The First Friend | The First Servant | The Giver of Good Food | The Wild Thing of the Woods
test: Leah: «it's giving villain, the Cat tricked the Woman with the mouse!» (it's giving = it seems like) Is that fair? | No: she asked him to eat it and thanked him herself | Yes, he brought the mouse in on purpose and let it go to frighten her | He never caught it | The Dog caught it
test: Why does the Man say «Not when I am near»? | The Cat still says he walks by himself | The Cat is sitting too near the Man's place by the fire, and the Man wants it back | The Man is afraid | The Cat asks to go hunting
test: What would have happened if the Cat had NOT added «still I am the Cat who walks by himself»? | The Man would have put his things away, and the Dog shut his mouth | Nothing different | The Woman would have thrown things | The Cat would have left
test: Which is TRUE in this part? | The Woman calls the Cat «as clever as a man» | The Man makes a Singing Magic with the blade-bone to keep the Cat out of the Cave | The Dog catches the mouse | The Cat refuses all bargains
test: Kipling says three proper Men out of five throw things at cats. What should we say today? | It's cruel and wrong | It's a good old rule, because cats who don't catch mice deserve a fright now and then | It's the law to throw boots | Only on Sundays
test: Real life: how did cats come to live with people? | Wildcats came for the mice in farmers' grain | A Woman made a bargain with one Cat in a Cave, and all cats have kept it ever since | They were caught in nets | Dogs brought them
test: Real life: about when did cats start living near farmers in the Middle East? | About 10,000 years ago | Last year | A million years ago | 100 years ago
test: Real life: which ancient people loved cats so much they made statues of a cat-goddess? | The ancient Egyptians | The Vikings, who took cats on their long ships across the sea | The Romans | The Tribe of Tegumai
test: Mason from Toronto: «dogs and cats always fight, eh?» (eh = right?) What is true? | No: many are good friends | Yes, always and everywhere, because of the old bargain in the Cave | Only on Sundays | Only big dogs
test: Looking back: in part 2, what fell when the Woman spoke the first word of praise? | The horse-skin curtain | The smoke from the fire at the back of the Cave, in clouds | The milk-pot | The Bat
test: Looking back: what were the three sounds of the three magic signs? | Whoosh, puff, ffft | Ahh, ohh, sshhh-sshhh | Bang, crash, thunder | Humph, humph, humph
test: Looking back: which earlier character also made a clever bargain that ended in a useful gift? | Pau Amma, who chose all the gifts | The Camel, who bargained with the Djinn for three days' rest | Painted Jaguar | The Stranger-man
test: Looking back: what did the Head Chief do at the end of the picture-letter muddle? | He laughed and called it a great invention | He threw his boots and his stone axe at Taffy and chased her up a tree | He wept | He left
test: Looking back: in the Elephant's Child, who was never touched by the Elephant's Child in his spanking spree? | Kolokolo Bird | The Baboon | The Ostrich | The Giraffe
test: Freddie from Wolverhampton: «bostin' story, the Dog's the Cat's best mate at the end.» (bostin' = brilliant) Put it right. | The Dog chases him up a tree | Right, they're best mates, and they share the warm white milk and the roast bones every evening | The Dog makes milk for him | The Dog leaves the Cave
test: ★★★ Counting: «two leather boots and his little stone axe (that makes three) ... a piece of wood and a hatchet (that is five)». If he threw only the boots and the axe, how many things were left in the row? | Two | Three | Five | None
test: ★★★ The Woman makes a Magic «that should prevent her from saying a third word». Why is it funny that the Still Magic itself brings the mouse? | Her own magic made the stillness that let the mouse out, so she praises the Cat after all | The mouse is magic | The Cat made the Magic | It isn't funny
test: ★★★ Careful: the Cat makes bargains with how many people or animals in the whole story? | Three | One: the Woman, because the Man and the Dog only made threats | Two: the Man and the Dog | Four, with the Bat
exam: Identify the occurrence which caused the Woman to utter a third word in praise of the Cat. | The swift capture of a mouse which had alarmed her | The Cat's singing to the Baby | The Cat's playing with the spindle whorl | The Bat's report
exam: Ascertain the number of objects set in a row by the Man. | Five | Three | Only two | Seven
exam: It is given that the Cat declined to forgo his claim to walk by himself. Select the response most consistent with the conduct of the Dog thereafter. | He undertook to chase the Cat up a tree whenever they should meet | He withdrew to the Wet Wild Woods | He shared his bones with the Cat | He was reconciled with the Cat
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the real origin of the companionship between cats and people? | Wildcats were drawn to farmers' grain stores by mice, some ten thousand years ago | A single bargain was made in a Cave | Cats were tamed before dogs | Cats were introduced by the Romans
